From 8c7291236eb6ad42ac920a77f0e3070b7c47b4f3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:55:05 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] dma-buf: annotate the fence signalling critical section
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The load-bearing claim of this whole series is that everything from
fence publication (dma_resv_add_fence()) up to the fence signal takes no
lock and allocates no memory -- that is what makes it safe for the
percpu_ref release callback to run from arbitrary context, including a
driver's hardware completion IRQ, and it is what avoids the deadlock
class Christian König and Matthew Brost described on v4 (a GFP_KERNEL
allocation on the signalling path can cycle back through reclaim and
wait on the very fence it is blocking).

That claim has so far only been asserted in comments. Wrap
dma_buf_io_map_active_release() in dma_fence_begin_signalling() /
dma_fence_end_signalling() so CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING checks it on every
boot, including on paths nobody thought to exercise by hand -- abort,
cancellation, the various nvme setup-failure rollbacks that route
through nvme_dmabuf_put_active() and can trigger this callback inline
from within percpu_ref_put().

This deliberately does not extend to nvme's timeout/reset/PCI-recovery
escalation, which can also sit on the path to the last active put in the
case where hardware never completes a request normally. Those paths are
shared with every non-dma-buf request nvme handles and are not ours to
place inside a fence signalling section. Whether they stay
allocation-free is a real open question -- nvme_reset_work() does
allocate -- and is tracked separately as a bounded-progress audit, not
asserted here. Annotating only the cheap, already-understood end and
treating that as proof the whole path is safe would be the same
overclaiming mistake as an earlier draft of this series made when it
first added the dedicated unmap workqueue.

Signed-off-by: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
---
 drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
index e2db606c3d2a..846c9cda7f61 100644
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
@@ -142,6 +142,27 @@ static void dma_buf_io_map_release_work(struct work_struct *work)
 static void dma_buf_io_map_active_release(struct percpu_ref *ref)
 {
 	struct dma_buf_io_map *map = container_of(ref, struct dma_buf_io_map, active);
+	bool cookie;
+
+	/*
+	 * The load-bearing claim of this design is that everything from
+	 * fence publication (dma_resv_add_fence() in dma_buf_io_drop_map())
+	 * up to this signal takes no lock and allocates nothing -- that is
+	 * what makes it safe to run from arbitrary context, including a
+	 * driver's hardware completion IRQ. That claim was previously only
+	 * asserted in comments. Annotate it so lockdep checks it on every
+	 * boot with CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING, including on paths nobody thought
+	 * to test by hand (abort, cancellation, the various nvme
+	 * setup-failure rollbacks that call nvme_dmabuf_put_active()).
+	 *
+	 * This does NOT cover nvme's timeout/reset/PCI-recovery escalation,
+	 * which can also sit on the path to the last active put. Those paths
+	 * are shared with every non-dma-buf request nvme handles and are not
+	 * ours to place inside a fence signalling section; whether they stay
+	 * allocation-free is tracked separately as an open question, not
+	 * asserted here.
+	 */
+	cookie = dma_fence_begin_signalling();
 
 	switch (map->release_mode) {
 	case DMA_BUF_IO_RELEASE_SYNC:
@@ -151,7 +172,7 @@ static void dma_buf_io_map_active_release(struct percpu_ref *ref)
 		 * published for this generation.
 		 */
 		complete(&map->drain);
-		return;
+		break;
 	case DMA_BUF_IO_RELEASE_FENCED:
 	default:
 		/*
@@ -163,7 +184,10 @@ static void dma_buf_io_map_active_release(struct percpu_ref *ref)
 		dma_fence_signal(map->fence);
 		INIT_WORK(&map->release_work, dma_buf_io_map_release_work);
 		queue_work(dma_buf_io_wq, &map->release_work);
+		break;
 	}
+
+	dma_fence_end_signalling(cookie);
 }
 
 int dma_buf_io_init_map(struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx, struct dma_buf_io_map *map)
-- 
